        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          Human-in-the-loop (HITL) tests play a critical role in the development and certification of space systems. An important aspect of planning a HITL test is determining the appropriate sample size needed for the test. While NASA requirements often specify number of participants required for verification, several recent major spaceflight programs have approached NASA with a request to use fewer subjects to certify their spacecraft, and NASA management has agreed. However, the NASA Human Health and Performance Subject Matter Expert community is concerned about this trend. The proposed study seeks to provide spacecraft verification-relevant data to NASA to better inform decisions about sample size minimums.<div>
This project had the following specific aims:
1. Understand what sample size rules-of-thumb are available in the literature, and how they may or may not apply to spaceflight HITL verifications.
2. Perform knowledge elicitation with relevant internal and external experts on sample size for the verification outcomes selected for the lab study.
3. Identify/develop high-fidelity NASA-representative systems for the study.
    • Partner with the Rapid Prototyping Laboratory to replicate recently completed Orion display verification tests
4. Investigate how sample size impacts data characteristics for selected verification outcomes, systems, and tasks.</div><div><br></div><div>Approach</div><div><br></div><div>The investigators performed a systematic study to compare the impact that varying samples sizes have on selected HITL verification outcomes with NASA-representative systems. The research team also worked with a Biostatistician to perform a complementary modeling analysis. This study was composed of five groups with a total of 52. Crew-like participants were recruited from the NASA Test Subject Facility. Additionally, the US Space Force, US Air Force, and US Navy Test Pilot Schools may be used to recruit participants. During the first session demographics were collected, and measures of stress were be taken during pre- and post-test. The investigators consulted with the Behavioral Medicine (BMED) Discipline Scientist for recommendations. Participants completed standard verification outcome measures, like the Bedford Workload scale and the NASA Modified System Usability Scale (NMSUS), at the end of each assignment. The quantity of mistakes, task completion duration, and achievement were also recorded. A modeling analysis was conducted to assess how sample size can affect the probability of a particular result based on the laboratory study data to provide more information regarding the number of participants questioned utilizing an alternative approach.
<br></div><div>The projected duration of the project was 12 months.</div><div><br></div><div>Results</div><div><br></div><div>Results consist of analyzed data that vary in sample sizes (n = 3, n = 5, n = 10, n = 15, n = 32) to see how verification decisions may change. Data was analyzed using boostrap sampling analysis as well as with simulations.</div>
        </div>

